\section{Results}

\subsection{Large-scale validation across heterogeneous NIRS calibration problems}

The strongest AOM-PLS result in the refreshed benchmark is the ASLS branch
followed by compact AOM with 5-fold cross-validation.  Across the complete
seeds 0/1/2 robustness run, this variant reaches a median RMSEP/PLS ratio of
0.9623 across 53 paired datasets, with 37 wins after averaging RMSEP over
seeds.  The refreshed runtime table reports a median fit time of 1.65 seconds
for the same variant.  The result has two chemometric implications.  First, a
small bank of interpretable linear operators is enough to improve over a
standard PLS reference on a majority of datasets.  Second, ASLS remains
valuable because baseline correction cannot always be reproduced by strict
fixed linear operators alone.

The AOM-Ridge result remains more provisional.  In the current regenerated
paired table, the available overlap for the Blender selector versus
Ridge-HPO is still small: median RMSEP/Ridge-HPO ratio 0.905 with 4 wins on 4
paired datasets.  The earlier full-cohort tuned-Ridge result is retained only
as single-seed background evidence until the full headline selector rerun
completes.

