\subsection{Robustness to preprocessing search complexity}

The ablation pattern is also informative.  Compact banks were more stable than
larger response-deduplicated or family-expanded banks.  Cross-validation
selection was more reliable than a single holdout.  POP-style component-wise
selection and aggressive OSC branches produced interesting individual cases but
were not selected as headline results because they added variance or
instability.  This supports a conservative practical recommendation: start with
identity plus a compact set of smoothing, derivative and detrending operators,
and use nonlinear corrections such as ASLS as explicitly separated branches.

The fact that larger operator banks did not systematically improve
external-test performance is not a weakness of AOM but an important analytical
finding: preprocessing selection is itself a source of model-selection
variance.  Compact operator banks provide a better bias--variance compromise
for routine NIRS calibration because they reduce the number of redundant
candidate filters competing for the same validation signal.

The refreshed selector diagnostics support this compact-bank position:
the three most frequently selected strict operators
(\texttt{sg\_smooth\_w21\_p3}, \texttt{sg\_d1\_w21\_p3} and
\texttt{sg\_d1\_w11\_p2}) account for 54.7\% of compact-bank selections, and
the compact bank beats the production default on 34 of 53 paired datasets.
The AOM-Ridge selector table is still sparse because the current top5\_fast
run does not emit Blender/AutoSelect diagnostics.

\input{tables/table_selector_diagnostics.tex}

\subsection{Operator-adaptive Ridge as a complementary calibration model}

AOM-Ridge gives a second instantiation of the same operator-adaptive principle.
The strongest deployable variant in the earlier single-seed evidence source
was the Blender selector, with AutoSelect and the global compact variant close
behind.  The refreshed multi-seed full-cohort run currently covers only the
faster top5\_fast variants, while Blender and AutoSelect remain supported by
the earlier full-cohort single-seed table plus the 20-dataset audit run.  This
indicates that operator-adaptive Ridge is promising, but less mature than the
compact AOM-PLS pipeline as a deployment claim.

The stronger AOM-Ridge numbers are oracle-envelope quantities.  In the master
oracle-by-model-class view, AOM-Ridge has a median RMSEP/PLS ratio of
approximately 0.942 and wins on 49 of 58 datasets.  In the 52-dataset
publication-table cohort, the oracle envelope improves over tuned Ridge by a
median 4.73\% and wins on 45 of 52 datasets.  Against PLS, CatBoost and CNN-1D,
the corresponding oracle-envelope median deltas are -7.73\%, -13.27\% and
-12.56\%, respectively.

Oracle results are reported only to quantify the potential headroom of the
operator-kernel formulation and are not used to support deployable performance
claims.  The central point is that Ridge, although less standard than PLS in
routine chemometrics, becomes a useful and interpretable competitor when the
spectral geometry is allowed to adapt through operator kernels.

\subsection{Classification as secondary validation}

The classification cohort of 16 binary/multi-class NIRS datasets was used to
test whether the operator-adaptive idea generalises beyond regression.  Two
families of AOM classifiers were benchmarked with seeds 0/1/2: AOM-PLS-DA
(global vs per-component selection, NIPALS-adjoint vs SIMPLS-covariance
engines) and AOM-Ridge classification (global, branch-global, superblock,
active-superblock and multi-block kernel variants on the compact bank).

The AOM-PLS-DA SIMPLS variants significantly outperform the PLS-DA
reference: AOM-PLS-DA global SIMPLS gains a median $+0.159$ balanced
accuracy (12/13 paired wins, Wilcoxon $p_\mathrm{Holm}=0.007$), and
POP-PLS-DA SIMPLS gains $+0.052$ (11/13 wins, $p_\mathrm{Holm}=0.018$).
The NIPALS-adjoint variants are noisier and do not reach the Holm gate.

The AOM-Ridge classification family produces an even stronger and more
uniform signal: every one of the five variants gains
between $+0.163$ and $+0.175$ balanced accuracy on the 14-dataset cohort
overlap, with 13--14 wins out of 14 and Wilcoxon $p_\mathrm{Holm}<0.001$
for every comparison.  The global, branch-global, superblock,
active-superblock and multi-kernel variants therefore all clear the Holm
gate by a wide margin against the PLS-DA reference.  AOM-Ridge
classification reaches a median absolute balanced accuracy around $0.61$
versus $0.45$ for PLS-DA on the same datasets.

Classification is reported here as a secondary validation axis rather
than as the primary contribution.  Both AOM families nonetheless provide
a clear cross-task signal that operator adaptation generalizes beyond
regression, and the AOM-Ridge classification result is particularly clean
given that all five Ridge variants improve simultaneously and significantly.

