-selection is implemented through covariance identities that enable fast NIPALS
-and SIMPLS variants while preserving original-wavelength coefficients.  For
-Ridge, operator-adaptive kernels provide a dual formulation with recoverable
-original-space coefficients.  The approach was evaluated on a heterogeneous
-benchmark of more than 50 NIRS datasets and compared with conventional PLS,
-Ridge, CatBoost and CNN baselines under documented search budgets.
+The framework is instantiated for PLS, Ridge and FastAOM.  AOM-PLS uses
+operator identities on cross-covariances to select spectral operators while
+recovering original-wavelength coefficients.  AOM-Ridge uses
+operator-induced kernels to adapt the regularized spectral geometry.  FastAOM
+extends the same paradigm to chain exploration through low-rank screening and
+sparse multi-kernel fitting.  The methods were evaluated on a heterogeneous
+local NIRS benchmark with 61 regression rows in the manifest, 50--53 paired
+datasets for the main AOM/FastAOM regression comparisons, and secondary
+classification validation.
 
