+\texttt{bench/scenarios/runs/paper\_aom\_linear\_hpo\_full\_cartesian\_*}.
+The classification workspaces are
+\texttt{bench/AOM\_v0/benchmark\_runs/paper\_aom\_aompls\_da\_seeds012} and
+\texttt{bench/AOM\_v0/Ridge/benchmark\_runs/paper\_aom\_aomridge\_cls\_seeds012}.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-Operator-adaptive calibration converts preprocessing selection from an external
-trial-and-error pipeline search into an internal, auditable component of the
-NIRS calibration model.  For PLS, the covariance identity
-$(\X\A_b\transpose)\transpose\Y=\A_b\X\transpose\Y$ enables efficient NIPALS
-and SIMPLS implementations while preserving original-space coefficients.  For
-Ridge, the same idea becomes a sum of operator-induced geometries in the dual.
-On the available heterogeneous NIRS benchmark, compact AOM-PLS and AOM-Ridge
-provide competitive linear calibrations with substantially simpler final
-models than large preprocessing-HPO workflows.  The practical contribution is
-a faster, more traceable and more deployable route for NIRS analytical method
-development, especially when calibration must be updated or audited in routine
-use.
+Operator-adaptive calibration reframes NIRS preprocessing selection as a
+model-internal calibration problem.  AOM-PLS embeds linear operators through
+cross-covariance identities, AOM-Ridge embeds them through operator-induced
+kernels, and FastAOM extends the idea to screened chain and sparse
+multi-kernel exploration.  Across the available heterogeneous benchmark, the
+approach improves over standard linear references, remains competitive with
+much more expensive preprocessing-HPO baselines on their completed overlaps,
+and preserves auditable linear calibration objects.  The result is not a
+single universal preprocessing recipe, but a practical optimization layer for
+NIRS method development.
 
