 \begin{abstract}
 Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) is a rapid and non-destructive analytical
-technique, but the development of reliable calibration models remains strongly
-dependent on spectral preprocessing.  In routine workflows, preprocessing is
-commonly selected through large external pipeline searches, which are
-computationally costly, statistically unstable on small calibration sets, and
-difficult to audit.  Here, we introduce operator-adaptive calibration, a
-framework that embeds linear preprocessing selection inside the calibration
-model itself.  Candidate spectral treatments are represented as linear
-operators acting on the spectra, while nonlinear or sample-adaptive corrections
-such as SNV, MSC and ASLS are handled as fold-local branches to avoid leakage.
+technique, but reliable calibration still depends strongly on spectral
+preprocessing.  Conventional workflows treat preprocessing as an external
+search over filters, scatter corrections, baseline corrections and model
+hyperparameters.  This paper presents operator-adaptive calibration, a
+framework that moves strict linear preprocessing alternatives inside the
+calibration model while retaining fold-local handling for fitted or
+sample-adaptive corrections such as SNV, MSC and ASLS.
 
-The framework is instantiated for PLS and Ridge regression.  For PLS, operator
-selection is implemented through covariance identities that enable fast NIPALS
-and SIMPLS variants while preserving original-wavelength coefficients.  For
-Ridge, operator-adaptive kernels provide a dual formulation with recoverable
-original-space coefficients.  The approach was evaluated on a heterogeneous
-benchmark of more than 50 NIRS datasets and compared with conventional PLS,
-Ridge, CatBoost and CNN baselines under documented search budgets.
+The framework is instantiated for PLS, Ridge and FastAOM.  AOM-PLS uses
+operator identities on cross-covariances to select spectral operators while
+recovering original-wavelength coefficients.  AOM-Ridge uses
+operator-induced kernels to adapt the regularized spectral geometry.  FastAOM
+extends the same paradigm to chain exploration through low-rank screening and
+sparse multi-kernel fitting.  The methods were evaluated on a heterogeneous
+local NIRS benchmark with 61 regression rows in the manifest, 50--53 paired
+datasets for the main AOM/FastAOM regression comparisons, and secondary
+classification validation.
 
-Compact operator-adaptive PLS combined with ASLS branch preprocessing achieved
-a multi-seed median RMSEP/PLS ratio of 0.962 with 37 wins on 53 paired
-datasets.  In the current partial AOM-Ridge overlap, the Blender selector
-achieved a median RMSEP/Ridge-HPO ratio of 0.905 with 4 wins on 4 paired
-datasets; the full-cohort Blender/AutoSelect promotion remains pending.
-Beyond predictive accuracy, the proposed models reduce the dependence on large
-preprocessing-HPO campaigns, produce traceable operator choices, retain
-interpretable coefficients, and achieve seconds-scale fitting for compact
-AOM-PLS.  Operator-adaptive calibration therefore provides a practical route
-toward faster, more robust and auditable NIRS method development.
+ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5 achieved a median RMSEP/PLS-standard ratio of 0.962 on
+53 paired datasets with 37 wins and Holm-adjusted Wilcoxon $p=0.043$.
+AOMRidge-Blender achieved a median RMSEP/Ridge-default ratio of 0.913 on 52
+datasets with 44 wins and $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$, and remained favourable
+against the cartesian Ridge-HPO baseline on the 34-dataset overlap
+(ratio 0.956, 27 wins, $p=0.011$).  The best FastAOM variant,
+FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised, reached a median RMSEP/PLS-standard ratio of
+0.953 on 50 datasets with 35 wins, while the compact sparse-MKR variant gave
+nearly the same paired ratio at a median fit time of 2.48 s.  A full
+cartesian preprocessing-HPO baseline was usable on 36 PLS and 35 Ridge
+datasets with three successful seeds; it sharply increased compute cost and
+reduced the AOM-PLS margin, whereas AOM-Ridge and FastAOM retained favourable
+median ratios on their overlaps.  Operator-adaptive calibration therefore
+offers a practical route to faster, more auditable and still interpretable
+NIRS method development.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} near-infrared spectroscopy; analytical calibration;
@@ -91,258 +96,185 @@
 
 Near-infrared spectroscopy is widely used as a rapid, non-destructive
 analytical technique for food, agriculture, plant phenotyping and process
-control \citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  Yet the development of
-reliable calibration models remains a major bottleneck.  In routine practice,
-predictive performance often depends less on the regression algorithm itself
-than on the selection of spectral preprocessing.  This selection is usually
-performed as an external pipeline search over smoothing, derivatives, scatter
-correction, baseline correction and scaling choices.  As a result, calibration
-development can become computationally expensive, statistically unstable on
-small calibration sets, and difficult to audit.
+control \citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  Its practical bottleneck
+is often not spectral acquisition but calibration development.  A small change
+in smoothing, derivative order, scatter correction or baseline correction can
+change a PLS model more than changing the regression algorithm itself.  In
+routine chemometric workflows, these choices are usually made through an
+external preprocessing and hyperparameter search.
 
-We argue that preprocessing selection should be treated as a core part of
-analytical calibration rather than as an external hyperparameter search.  This
-is particularly important for NIRS, where calibration models are frequently
-deployed across heterogeneous matrices, traits, instruments and sample sizes.
-Preprocessing selection conditions not only predictive performance, but also
-external robustness, reproducibility, development cost, transferability to
-non-expert users and the auditability of the final calibration object.
+That workflow is familiar, but it is expensive and difficult to audit.
+Preprocessing is a modelling decision: it controls which spectral directions
+are visible to the regressor, how nuisance variation is suppressed, and which
+coefficient profile is ultimately deployed.  Treating this decision as a
+large external grid or Optuna search can be defensible in a benchmark, but it
+also increases development time and selection variance, especially in
+small-$n$ NIRS settings where validation folds are noisy.
 
-The novelty of the present work is therefore not another preprocessing filter.
-It is a change in the calibration paradigm: preprocessing alternatives are
-treated as structured model components with recoverable coefficients, rather
-than as competing black-box pipelines.  We call this approach
-operator-adaptive calibration.  Strict linear spectral treatments are encoded
-as a bank of operators $\{\A_1,\ldots,\A_m\}$ acting on the spectra, and the
-calibration model selects among them internally.  The identity operator is
-always included, so standard PLS or Ridge remains available.  Nonlinear or
-sample-adaptive corrections such as SNV, MSC and ASLS are kept as explicit
-fold-local branches to avoid leakage and to preserve the distinction between
-linear operator algebra and fitted preprocessing.
+We propose a different calibration paradigm.  Strict linear preprocessing
+alternatives are represented as a finite operator bank
+$\mathcal A=\{\A_1,\ldots,\A_m\}$ and selected inside the calibration model.
+The identity operator is always included, so the standard model remains
+available.  Sample-adaptive or fitted transformations such as SNV, MSC, EMSC
+and ASLS are not forced into this algebra; they remain explicit fold-local
+branches.  The resulting calibration object records which operator geometry
+was selected and still maps original spectra to responses through
+original-wavelength coefficients.
 
