-These ablations support three practical conclusions.  First, the compact bank
-already matches the 100-operator production/default bank at the median while
-using roughly one tenth of the operator/component candidates.  Second, the
-largest gain comes from replacing deterministic holdout selection with
-fold-based selection: compact CV-5 improves from the production/default
-median near 0.999 to 0.992 without any ASLS branch, and repeated CV-3 reaches
-0.984.  Third, adding the ASLS branch before compact AOM-PLS gives the current
-headline variant, but the nearby 15-op and 46--47-op bank results show that
-the conclusion is not a one-off artifact of one tiny bank.  The compact bank
-is selected as the deployable default because it lies on the accuracy/time
-frontier: ASLS + response-dedup with SPXY can be marginally close, but it is
-substantially slower and has fewer paired rows.
+The heatmap highlights three patterns.  First, derivative operators dominate
+many trait groups where local spectral contrast is useful.  Second, detrending
+and finite-difference operators appear as dataset-specific alternatives rather
+than global defaults.  Third, datasets that do not benefit from the compact
+bank are not concentrated in one family; they occur when the default wider
+bank or a branch correction better matches the validation split.  Quantitatively,
+compact AOM-PLS beats the production default on 34 of 53 paired datasets, and
+strict compact AOM-PLS beats the ASLS branch on 23 of 53 datasets.  The ASLS
+branch is therefore useful but not universally dominant.
 
