-\section{Algorithmic Sketches}
-
-\subsection{Global AOM-PLS}
-
-\begin{enumerate}
-  \item Build a compact operator bank containing identity and a small set of
-  smoothing, derivative and detrending operators.
-  \item For each candidate number of components and each operator, evaluate
-  calibration-fold performance.
-  \item Select the operator and component count with the best calibration-fold
-  criterion.
-  \item Refit on the complete calibration set.
-  \item Store selected operator, component count, original-space coefficients,
-  centering constants and fold-level diagnostics.
-\end{enumerate}
+\subsection{FastAOM chain grammar and simplification}
 
-\subsection{AOM-Ridge}
+FastAOM defines a chain as an ordered composition of primitive strict
+operators.  The grammar assigns roles to operators: baseline/projection,
+smoothing, derivative and projection-mask operations.  A depth cap limits the
+number of composed operators.  The compact runs use depth 3 for most variants,
+while the depth-4 hard-chain diagnostic completed on one dataset.  Grammar
+pruning removes invalid or redundant role sequences before screening.  The
+simplifier keeps only algebraically safe reductions inherited from the parent
+AOM implementation, such as identity removal and consecutive detrend collapse;
+it does not collapse different smoothers or remove valid derivative-boundary
+chains.
 
-\begin{enumerate}
-  \item Build operator-induced kernels
-  $\K_b=\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose$ inside the calibration fold.
-  \item Select $\alpha$ and operator geometry by nested calibration-fold
-  validation.
-  \item Solve the dual Ridge system.
-  \item Recover original-space coefficients.
-  \item Refit on the complete calibration set and evaluate once on the external
-  test set.
-\end{enumerate}
+For each fold-local base $B_j(\X)$ and chain $s$, FastAOM computes the
+screening score
+\begin{equation}
+  \mathrm{score}(j,s)
+  =
+  \frac{\left\|A_s B_j(\X_c)\transpose y_c\right\|_2^2}
+       {\left\|B_j(\X_c)A_s\transpose\right\|_F^2
+        \left\|y_c\right\|_2^2}.
+\end{equation}
+The denominator is estimated from a truncated SVD
+$B_j(\X_c)\approx U_j\Sigma_jV_j\transpose$.  With
+$F_{j,s}=A_sV_j\Sigma_j$,
+\begin{equation}
+  \K_{j,s}\approx U_jC_{j,s}U_j\transpose,
+  \qquad
+  C_{j,s}=F_{j,s}\transpose F_{j,s}.
+\end{equation}
+This gives low-rank kernel-vector products during screening and permits
+diversity-aware top-$k$ filtering by base and operator family.
 
